        "q": "You are a UX writer at a fintech company. A new user opens the app for the first time and sees a blank dashboard with no transactions. Write the empty state copy for this screen.",
        "a": "Empty states are one of the highest-leverage moments in UX writing because they speak to users who are uncertain and need both reassurance and a clear next step.\n\nFor a new fintech user with no transactions, the empty state needs to accomplish three things: confirm the user is in the right place, remove anxiety about why nothing is showing, and give a single clear action.\n\nA strong empty state for this screen:\n\nHeadline: Your financial picture starts here\nBody: Once you add an account or make your first transaction, you'll see everything in one place.\nCTA button: Add your first account\n\nThe headline is affirming rather than apologetic. It does not say \"Nothing here yet\" which centers on absence. It centers on potential.\n\nThe body copy does two things simultaneously: explains what will appear here, and sets the expectation that the user needs to take action to make it happen. It does not say \"No transactions found\" because this user has not made any — \"found\" implies something was looked for and not discovered, which is inaccurate and mildly alarming for a new user.\n\nThe CTA is singular and specific. Not \"Get started\" which is vague. Not two buttons which creates decision paralysis. One action that moves the user forward.\n\nFor accessibility and localization, I keep the copy short enough that it does not truncate on smaller screens and avoid idioms that do not translate well across Indian languages if the app supports multiple languages.",
