        "q": "You are a UX writer at a banking app. The user's payment has failed because their account has insufficient funds. Write the error message. What do you consider before writing?",
        "a": "Error messages in banking apps are high-stakes moments. The user is already frustrated and potentially anxious about their finances. A poorly written error can damage trust, create confusion, or cause the user to abandon the flow entirely.\n\nBefore writing, I consider four things. What the user was trying to do. What went wrong and why. What they can do next. And what emotional state they are likely in.\n\nFor an insufficient funds error, the user was trying to make a payment. They know what they were doing. What they need is confirmation of what went wrong, without jargon or blame, and a specific path forward.\n\nA strong error message:\n\nHeadline: Payment unsuccessful\nBody: Your account balance is too low to complete this payment. Add money to your account or try a different payment method.\nSecondary action: Check balance\n\nI avoid several things in this copy. I do not write \"Transaction declined\" because \"declined\" implies rejection by an authority, which feels stigmatizing. I do not write \"Insufficient funds\" as the headline because it is cold, technical, and leads with the problem rather than the situation. I do not write \"You don't have enough money\" because it is direct to the point of being unkind.\n\nI also avoid the passive construction \"The payment could not be processed\" which removes agency from the situation and gives the user nothing to act on. Every error message in a financial product should end with a clear next step.\n\nThe secondary action to check balance is useful because the user may not know their balance and needs that information to decide what to do next.",
