        "q": "You are a UX writer at an insurance company. You need to write the onboarding tooltip copy for a new feature that lets users track their claim status in real time. The product manager wants to call it 'Live Claim Tracker'. How do you handle this?",
        "a": "Feature naming requests from product managers are a common friction point for UX writers because PMs often name features from an internal product perspective rather than a user perspective.\n\n'Live Claim Tracker' has a specific problem: it is not wrong, but it prioritizes the product's capability over the user's experience. Users do not think about tracking — they think about knowing. They want to know where their claim is, not track it.\n\nI would have a conversation with the PM before accepting the name as fixed. My approach would be to present data or examples, not just a preference. I would ask: when users call the helpline about a claim, what do they say? They say 'I want to know where my claim is' or 'Can you tell me what's happening with my claim' — not 'I want to track my claim.'\n\nFor the tooltip copy, regardless of what the feature is called internally, the user-facing copy should speak to what the user gets, not what the feature does.\n\nTooltip headline: Know exactly where your claim stands\nTooltip body: See real-time updates on your claim — from submission to settlement — without calling us.\n\nThis copy leads with the user benefit (knowing), specifies the scope (submission to settlement), and adds a secondary benefit (no need to call). It does not use the word 'tracker' because the user is not tracking — they are being informed.\n\nIf the PM insists on the feature name for branding or development reasons, I accept it as the product name while ensuring the user-facing copy in the tooltip and the feature itself always speaks in user language.",
