        "q": "You are a UX writer at a telecom company. Design the microcopy for a three-step SIM card activation flow. The steps are: verify identity, confirm plan, activate SIM. Each step needs a headline and a one-line body.",
        "a": "A three-step activation flow needs microcopy that does three jobs simultaneously: tells the user where they are, tells them what they need to do, and keeps momentum moving forward. The copy should feel like a guided conversation, not a form.\n\nStep 1 — Verify Identity\nHeadline: Let's confirm who you are\nBody: We need a quick identity check to keep your account secure.\n\nThe headline uses \"Let's\" to make it collaborative and conversational. It does not say \"Identity Verification Required\" which is bureaucratic and creates friction. The body gives a reason — security — which builds trust and explains why the step exists.\n\nStep 2 — Confirm Plan\nHeadline: Your plan, your way\nBody: Check your selected plan before we lock it in — you can still change it here.\n\nThe headline acknowledges agency. The body is important because it proactively addresses a common user anxiety: can I change my mind? Answering this question before the user asks it reduces drop-off.\n\nStep 3 — Activate SIM\nHeadline: One tap to go live\nBody: Your SIM will activate within 2 minutes. Keep your phone nearby.\n\nThe headline celebrates the final step with positive energy. The body sets a specific expectation (2 minutes, not \"shortly\" or \"soon\") and gives a practical instruction (keep your phone nearby) that prevents the user from panicking if nothing happens immediately.\n\nAcross all three steps I maintain a consistent second-person voice, avoid passive constructions, and keep each body line under twelve words so it reads comfortably at a glance on a mobile screen.",
