        "q": "You are a UX writer at a fintech startup. The legal team wants to add a 15-line disclaimer to the loan application screen. How do you handle this request?",
        "a": "Legal copy requests are one of the most common and most important negotiations a UX writer navigates. The legal team has a legitimate obligation to disclose information and protect the company. The UX writer has a legitimate obligation to protect the user experience and ensure users actually understand what they are agreeing to. These goals are not inherently in conflict.\n\nMy first step is to understand what the disclaimer is trying to accomplish. I read all 15 lines and identify the core obligations: what must be disclosed, what cannot be omitted, and what was included out of legal habit rather than genuine necessity.\n\nI then propose a restructured approach to the legal team. I do not go to the meeting asking to remove content — I go asking to make the content more effective. A 15-line disclaimer that no user reads has zero legal protection value. A clearly structured disclosure that users can actually parse has real value.\n\nFor the loan application screen specifically, I would propose a layered approach. On the screen: a two-line summary of the most important disclosure with a link reading \"See full loan terms.\" Tapping the link opens a modal or separate screen with the complete disclosure formatted for readability — short paragraphs, headers, not a wall of text.\n\nThis approach gives legal everything they need in terms of disclosure while giving users a path to understand it if they want to. I would document the legal team's approval of this approach in writing.\n\nThe one thing I would not do is simply place all 15 lines on the screen in a small font and consider the job done. That is legal theater, not compliance, and it fails both the user and the company.",
