        "q": "You are a UX writer at a banking app. Write the push notification copy for three scenarios: a successful transaction, a payment due reminder, and a suspicious activity alert. What principles guide you?",
        "a": "Push notifications are the most intrusive format in mobile UX because they interrupt whatever the user is doing. The principles that guide notification copy are: earn the interruption, be specific not vague, and give the user something to do or know.\n\nScenario 1 — Successful transaction\nTitle: Payment sent ✓\nBody: ₹2,500 to Rahul Kumar. Your balance: ₹18,340.\n\nThe checkmark does real work here — it provides instant visual confirmation before the user reads the text. I include specific amounts rather than \"Your payment was processed successfully\" because specificity builds confidence and allows the user to verify without opening the app.\n\nScenario 2 — Payment due reminder\nTitle: EMI due in 3 days\nBody: ₹8,200 due on 15 July. Tap to pay now and avoid a late fee.\n\nThe urgency is specific (3 days, not \"soon\") and the consequence is named (late fee) without being threatening. The CTA is embedded in the body because Android and iOS limit how many action buttons appear. I lead with the timeline because that is what drives action.\n\nScenario 3 — Suspicious activity alert\nTitle: Unusual activity on your account\nBody: A transaction we don't recognize. Tap to review and secure your account.\n\nThis is the highest stakes notification. I do not use the word \"fraud\" because it creates panic before the user has reviewed anything. \"Unusual activity\" is accurate and appropriately serious without being alarming. The CTA gives two things — review and secure — so the user knows they have agency and can take action immediately.\n\nAcross all three, I keep titles under 40 characters and bodies under 70 characters so they do not get truncated on lock screens.",
