        "q": "You are a content designer at an insurance company. A feature lets users compare two health insurance plans side by side. Write the comparison table headers and helper text for four key attributes: premium, coverage, network hospitals, and waiting period.",
        "a": "Comparison tables in insurance products fail users when they use technical terminology that means different things to different people. My job as a content designer is to translate insurance language into terms that help users make real decisions.\n\nPremium\nHeader: Monthly cost\nHelper text: What you pay every month to keep this plan active.\n\n\"Premium\" is industry jargon. Most users understand \"monthly cost\" immediately. The helper text adds the crucial word \"active\" — it clarifies that this is the ongoing cost, not a one-time payment, which is a common source of confusion.\n\nCoverage\nHeader: What's covered\nHelper text: The medical expenses this plan will pay for, up to the sum insured.\n\nCoverage is slightly less jargon-heavy but still vague. \"What's covered\" is direct and action-oriented. The helper text introduces \"sum insured\" because it is a term users will see in the full policy — I am gently introducing them to it rather than avoiding it entirely.\n\nNetwork hospitals\nHeader: Hospitals near you\nHelper text: Get cashless treatment at these partner hospitals — no bills to submit.\n\nThis is the most behaviorally important attribute for most users. \"Network hospitals\" sounds like an IT term. \"Hospitals near you\" connects it to the user's actual situation. The helper text explains the benefit of cashless treatment in a way that makes the value of a large network tangible.\n\nWaiting period\nHeader: When coverage kicks in\nHelper text: Pre-existing conditions are covered after this period. New conditions are covered immediately.\n\nWaiting period is often misunderstood — users sometimes think it means the plan does not work for this long. The header reframes it as a timeline to benefit. The helper text distinguishes between pre-existing and new conditions because this distinction drives real purchase decisions.",
