        "q": "You are a UX writer at a telecom company. The app needs to ask users for permission to access their location. Write the permission request copy for both iOS and Android. What do you need to know before writing?",
        "a": "Before writing permission request copy, I need to know four things: what the feature actually uses location for, whether location access is required or optional for the feature to work, whether the use is ongoing or one-time, and what the user gets in exchange for granting permission.\n\nFor a telecom app, location is most likely used to show nearby stores, recommend plans based on coverage in the area, or provide network status for the user's current location. I need to know which specific use case applies because this directly changes the copy.\n\nAssuming the use case is showing nearby stores and network coverage:\n\niOS (one dialog, limited copy space):\nTitle: Allow access to your location?\nBody: InterviewAlpha uses your location to show nearby stores and check network coverage in your area.\n\nAndroid (more control over copy):\nTitle: Let us help you get the best experience nearby\nBody: We'll use your location to show you the closest stores and the network coverage available where you are. You can change this anytime in Settings.\n\nSeveral principles drive this copy. I lead with what the user gets, not what the app needs. I am specific about the use cases — \"nearby stores and network coverage\" rather than \"improve your experience\" which is vague and generates distrust. For Android, I add the reassurance that location access can be changed, which reduces the perceived commitment of granting permission.\n\nI avoid the common mistake of writing \"We need your location to provide services\" which sounds extractive. I also avoid \"We use your location data\" which introduces the word \"data\" unnecessarily and creates privacy anxiety.\n\nThe copy should be tested with users who have previously declined location permissions on other apps — they are the hardest audience and the most important to convert.",
