        "q": "You are a UX writer at a fintech company. A/B test results show your 'Transfer Money' button copy is underperforming against the control. The control says 'Send Money'. How do you analyze this and what do you recommend?",
        "a": "A/B test results showing that 'Send Money' outperforms 'Transfer Money' is not surprising and the analysis reveals something important about how users understand their own actions.\n\nThe word 'transfer' is a banking term. It describes what happens technically — a transfer of funds between accounts. The word 'send' describes what the user is doing from their perspective — sending money to a person. Users do not think of themselves as transferring. They think of themselves as sending.\n\nThis is the core principle at work: copy should reflect the user's mental model, not the product's internal model. Fintech products frequently use banking terminology because the people building them come from banking backgrounds. UX writers are the advocates for the user's perspective.\n\nMy recommendation has two parts. First, update the primary CTA to 'Send Money' based on the A/B test evidence. Second, audit the entire money movement flow for similar terminology issues. If the button said 'Transfer', the screens beyond it probably say 'Transfer amount', 'Transfer details', 'Transfer complete'. All of these should be rewritten in human language.\n\nI would also investigate what 'underperforming' means specifically. Is it a conversion rate difference, a time-to-tap difference, or a completion rate difference for the full flow? Each tells a different story. A conversion difference means users are not tapping. A time-to-tap difference means users are pausing to read and understand. A completion difference means they tap but abandon later, which suggests the flow after the button has copy problems too.\n\nI document this learning formally so future copy decisions in this product start from 'send' language rather than 'transfer' language by default.",
