        "q": "You are a content designer at a bank. The design team wants to add a progress indicator to a 5-step KYC verification flow. Write the label for each step and the success state message when the flow completes.",
        "a": "Progress indicator labels need to be short enough to fit in a limited space, clear enough that users know what to expect in each step, and written in the user's language rather than internal process terminology.\n\nKYC stands for Know Your Customer — this is internal terminology that should never appear in user-facing copy. Users are not completing KYC. They are verifying their identity to access the account.\n\nStep labels:\n1. Personal details\n2. Address proof\n3. Identity check\n4. Photo verification\n5. Review & confirm\n\nI chose these labels deliberately. \"Personal details\" rather than \"Basic information\" because it is more specific about what is being collected. \"Address proof\" is actually familiar to Indian users because it is commonly used in everyday contexts unlike \"Address verification\" which sounds clinical. \"Identity check\" rather than \"ID verification\" because \"check\" is more conversational and less bureaucratic. \"Photo verification\" rather than \"Selfie upload\" because some users may not be comfortable with the word selfie in a banking context. \"Review & confirm\" rather than \"Submit\" because it accurately describes what the user does on the final step — they review everything before confirming.\n\nSuccess state message:\nHeadline: You're all set\nBody: Your identity has been verified. Your account is now fully active and ready to use.\nCTA: Go to my account\n\nThe headline is celebratory without being excessive. The body provides two pieces of information: confirmation that the verification worked, and what this means for the user (fully active account). The CTA takes them directly to the value they signed up for.",
