        "q": "You are a UX writer at a fintech company. A modal asks users to confirm they want to close their account. This is irreversible. How do you write the modal copy?",
        "a": "Irreversible action confirmations are one of the most consequential pieces of microcopy in any product because they are the last line of defense between a user and an action they may regret.\n\nThe design principle for destructive confirmation modals is: communicate the consequence clearly without catastrophizing, make the confirm action require deliberate intent, and make the cancel action obviously easy.\n\nHere is how I would write this modal:\n\nHeadline: Close your account?\n\nBody: This will permanently close your account and delete all your data. Any remaining balance will be transferred to your linked bank account within 5-7 days. This cannot be undone.\n\nDanger button (right): Close account\nSafe button (left): Keep my account\n\nSeveral decisions here are deliberate. The headline is a question, not a statement. \"Close your account?\" is softer than \"Are you sure?\" (which is condescending) and clearer than \"Account Closure\" (which is a heading, not a confirmation).\n\nThe body copy does three specific things: names the permanent consequence (account closed, data deleted), addresses the most common anxiety about closing a fintech account (what happens to my money?), and ends with \"This cannot be undone\" as a final, brief statement that carries serious weight without using alarming capitalization.\n\nThe button labels are asymmetric and specific. \"Close account\" on the danger button is specific — the user has to read and confirm the actual action they are taking. \"Keep my account\" on the safe button gives the user a positive action, not a negative one. \"Cancel\" is weaker than \"Keep my account\" because it does not reinforce what the user is preserving by not proceeding.\n\nI would not use red text throughout the modal body — only the danger button should be red. Too much red creates panic rather than careful consideration.",
