        "q": "You are a content designer at an insurance company. The app needs to explain a complex insurance concept — co-payment — to users who are choosing between plans. How do you approach this?",
        "a": "Co-payment is a concept that confuses even financially literate users because the word does not intuitively convey what it means. My approach to explaining complex insurance concepts follows a three-layer model: plain language definition, concrete example, and consequence for the user's decision.\n\nPlain language definition:\nCo-payment means you share the cost of treatment with your insurer. When you make a claim, you pay a fixed percentage and your insurer pays the rest.\n\nThis definition does two things: it uses \"share\" which is immediately understandable, and it establishes the two parties (you and your insurer) so the subsequent explanation makes sense.\n\nConcrete example:\nFor example, with a 10% co-payment on a ₹1,00,000 hospital bill, you pay ₹10,000 and your insurer covers ₹90,000.\n\nThe example uses a round number that is easy to calculate mentally. I use \"hospital bill\" rather than \"claim amount\" because the user thinks about paying a hospital, not filing a claim. I show both amounts explicitly rather than asking the user to calculate 90% of anything.\n\nConsequence for decision:\nPlans with co-payment typically have lower monthly premiums. If you rarely need hospital care, this trade-off may save you money overall.\n\nThis is the content that most insurance apps omit. Users do not just need to understand what co-payment is — they need to know what it means for their specific situation. The consequence statement helps users self-select between plans based on their health usage rather than choosing based on which term they recognize.\n\nFor placement, I would put this in a tooltip or expandable section rather than on the main comparison screen. It should be available but not forced on users who already understand the concept.",
