        "q": "You are an AI content engineer at a telecom company. The AI assistant has been making the following type of error: when customers ask about international roaming, it sometimes gives outdated plan information. How do you diagnose and fix this?",
        "a": "Outdated plan information in an AI assistant is a content freshness problem, not a model problem. The AI is working correctly — it is accurately retrieving and presenting information from its knowledge base. The knowledge base is the problem.\n\nDiagnosis steps:\n\nFirst, I identify the specific knowledge base documents that contain the outdated roaming information. I compare the information the AI is providing against the current plan details to confirm it is a discrepancy between the knowledge base and the current reality, not a model hallucination.\n\nSecond, I examine the update history of those documents. When were they last updated? Was there a plan change that did not trigger a documentation update? Was the documentation update process not followed?\n\nThird, I check whether this is an isolated incident with roaming plans or a symptom of a broader documentation freshness problem across other plan categories.\n\nFixes:\n\nImmediate: Update the specific knowledge base documents that contain the outdated roaming information with current, accurate plan details. Verify the AI is retrieving the updated documents after the update.\n\nProcess fix: The root cause is almost always that plan changes and documentation updates are not linked in the workflow. The product or pricing team changed the plans without notifying the documentation team. I implement a change notification process — when plans are updated, documentation update is a mandatory step before the change goes live.\n\nPreventive: I add version dates and expiry markers to time-sensitive documentation. A document about roaming plans that has not been reviewed in 90 days should be flagged for review rather than trusted as current.\n\nMonitoring: I set up regular sampling of queries about roaming plans and compare the AI responses against the current published plans. This creates an ongoing check rather than relying on customers to report errors.",
