        "q": "You are an AI content designer at a fintech company. The company wants to use AI to personalize the onboarding experience based on user segment. A first-time investor and a seasoned trader should have different onboarding journeys. How do you design this?",
        "a": "Personalized AI onboarding based on user segment requires designing multiple conversation paths that feel tailored rather than segmented, and a detection or selection mechanism that routes users to the right path.\n\nDetection mechanism options:\n\nExplicit selection: Ask the user directly at the start of onboarding. \"How would you describe your investing experience?\" with three or four options. This is the most reliable approach and lets users self-identify. The disadvantage is that some users may not know which category applies to them.\n\nProgressive detection: Ask a single qualifying question that reveals the user's level without explicitly asking. \"Have you invested in mutual funds or stocks before?\" Yes/No. The user's answer routes them to the appropriate path without making them feel categorized.\n\nBehavioral inference: If the user has provided information during signup that reveals their segment — a field indicating years of investment experience, or connecting an existing demat account — use that to route without asking.\n\nPath design for first-time investor:\n\nThe first-time investor path prioritizes education alongside setup. The AI asks simpler questions, explains each step more thoroughly, uses analogies rather than financial jargon, and celebrates each completed step. The first question is about their goal (\"What are you hoping to achieve by investing?\") rather than their strategy.\n\nPath design for seasoned trader:\n\nThe seasoned trader path prioritizes speed and capability. It assumes knowledge of financial concepts, skips basic explanations, asks about specific preferences (order types, risk parameters, preferred markets), and surfaces advanced features faster. The tone is peer-to-peer rather than instructional.\n\nCross-path flexibility: Users should be able to switch paths if they end up in the wrong one. A \"take me back to basics\" option for a user who selected seasoned but is encountering unfamiliar terminology, and an \"I want to skip ahead\" option for the opposite situation.",
