        "q": "You are an AI content engineer at a bank. Explain what 'context window' means in a language model and how it affects the design of a banking AI assistant.",
        "a": "The context window is the maximum amount of text that a language model can process at one time — the model can only \"see\" and consider information that fits within this window when generating a response. Information outside the context window is simply not available to the model during that generation.\n\nA practical explanation:\n\nImagine the context window as a desk. The language model can only work with what is currently on the desk. If the desk is full and you need to add something new, something else must be removed. The model cannot access information that is not currently on the desk, even if it exists somewhere in a filing cabinet.\n\nContext windows are measured in tokens — roughly three to four characters per token, or about 0.75 words. A model with a 128,000 token context window can process roughly 100,000 words at once — equivalent to a short novel.\n\nHow this affects a banking AI assistant:\n\nConversation length: Every message in a conversation occupies context window space. In a long customer support conversation, earlier messages may eventually be pushed out of the context window, causing the model to \"forget\" information from the beginning of the conversation. This must be managed through summarization — periodically condensing earlier conversation history — or through explicit key information storage.\n\nKnowledge base retrieval: When a RAG system retrieves documents to ground AI responses, the retrieved documents must fit within the remaining context window after the conversation history and system prompt are included. For a banking assistant with a long system prompt and detailed conversation history, the available space for retrieved documents may be limited, requiring strategic decisions about which documents to retrieve and in how much detail.\n\nSystem prompt length: A comprehensive system prompt that includes many few-shot examples and detailed instructions occupies significant context window space. There is a trade-off between a thorough system prompt that guides the model's behavior and the available context for conversation and retrieved documents.\n\nFor a banking assistant, I manage context window constraints by keeping the system prompt as concise as possible, implementing conversation summarization at appropriate intervals, and optimizing retrieved document size through chunking strategy.",
