        "q": "You are an AI content engineer at a telecom company. You are asked to build a knowledge base for an AI assistant that will help customers troubleshoot their internet connection. How do you structure the content?",
        "a": "An internet troubleshooting knowledge base for a telecom AI assistant needs to be structured around how customers actually experience connection problems, not around the technical hierarchy of the network infrastructure.\n\nStructuring principles:\n\nSymptom-first organization: Customers describe their experience, not the cause. They say \"my internet is slow\" not \"I have a bandwidth congestion issue on the last mile.\" The knowledge base should be organized by symptom — slow internet, no connection, intermittent connection, WiFi working but ethernet not, specific device not connecting — with each symptom having its own diagnostic pathway.\n\nDecision tree structure within each symptom: For each symptom, the troubleshooting pathway should follow a decision tree that branches based on what the customer tells the AI. \"Can you connect to the router's admin page?\" Yes → one path. No → different path. This requires content authors to think through every branch, not just the common path.\n\nSelf-contained troubleshooting chunks: Each step in the troubleshooting process should be a self-contained content chunk that explains what to do, what the expected outcome is, and what to do if the outcome is not as expected. A chunk that says \"restart your router\" without explaining what to expect or what to do if restarting does not help is incomplete.\n\nDevice-specific variations: Internet troubleshooting steps differ by device type (Windows, Mac, Android, iOS) and by router model. The knowledge base needs to handle these variations without asking the AI to guess. Either explicit device-specific articles, or a structure where the common steps are in one article and device-specific steps are in linked supplementary articles.\n\nClear escalation criteria: The knowledge base must include explicit content about when to escalate to a human agent — after X steps without resolution, when the issue appears to be on the network side rather than the customer's equipment, when the customer expresses frustration at a defined threshold.",
        "subcategory": null,
        "difficulty": "Medium",
