        "q": "You are a content strategist at a fintech company. The company is preparing to launch and needs to decide between building a help center in-house or using a third-party platform like Zendesk or Intercom. How do you evaluate this decision?",
        "a": "A build versus buy decision for a help center platform is a strategic choice with long-term implications for the documentation team's productivity, the user experience, and the total cost of ownership. I evaluate it across five dimensions.\n\nFeature requirements fit:\n\nI inventory the features the team needs now and in the next two years: article authoring, search, user feedback collection, analytics, multilingual support, AI-powered search, and integration with the support ticket system. I compare these requirements against the platforms under consideration. Platforms like Zendesk Guide, Intercom Articles, and Notion cover most standard needs. Custom builds can cover all needs but require engineering resources to maintain every feature.\n\nTime to value:\n\nA third-party platform can be live in days to weeks with existing content. A custom build requires engineering time that may delay launch by months. For a company preparing for launch, time to value often tips the decision toward a third-party platform for the initial implementation.\n\nTotal cost of ownership:\n\nThird-party platform costs are visible: license fees, typically per agent per month. Custom build costs are less visible: engineering time to build, engineering time to maintain, and opportunity cost of engineering capacity that could be used for product features. I model both over a three-year period.\n\nData ownership and portability:\n\nFor a fintech company that may need to comply with data localization requirements, where article content and user behavior data is stored matters. Third-party platforms may store data outside India. I verify the platform's data storage locations and whether they meet applicable requirements.\n\nScalability:\n\nIf the company is planning to expand to multiple languages or multiple products, a platform with native multilingual and multi-brand support avoids the need to rebuild when scale changes. My recommendation for a launch-stage fintech: start with a third-party platform and defer the custom build decision until the team has clear evidence that the platform's limitations are costing them user experience quality or team productivity.",
