        "q": "You are a documentation manager at a bank. How do you handle a situation where two subject matter experts give you contradictory information about the same process?",
        "a": "Contradictory information from two subject matter experts is a common and important challenge in technical writing, because publishing incorrect information in regulated banking documentation creates compliance and operational risks.\n\nMy approach is structured rather than diplomatic.\n\nStep 1 — Document both versions precisely:\n\nBefore engaging either expert, I write down both versions of the information as specifically as possible. \"Expert A says the account freeze process requires authorization from the branch manager. Expert B says it can be done by the relationship manager without branch manager approval.\" Vague contradictions are hard to resolve; specific ones usually have a clear path to resolution.\n\nStep 2 — Identify the type of contradiction:\n\nIs this a factual contradiction about what the process currently is, or a process disagreement about what it should be? Is it a geographic or role-based difference — the process works differently in different branches or for different account types? Is it a change that has occurred and one expert knows the current process while the other knows the previous one? Each type has a different resolution path.\n\nStep 3 — Go to the authoritative source:\n\nFor a banking process, the authoritative source is not either expert's opinion but the documented policy or system configuration. I ask both experts: where is this process documented officially? I then look at that source. If no official documentation exists, that is itself a finding — the process has not been formally documented and needs to be.\n\nStep 4 — If the process is genuinely inconsistent:\n\nIf the process is genuinely inconsistent — different branches are doing it differently with no official standard — I document this as a process gap, not as a documentation ambiguity. The documentation I produce reflects the intended process as defined by the appropriate authority, with a note that current practice may vary.",
