        "q": "You are a content strategist at an insurance company. The company has decided to rewrite all its product documentation in plain language. How do you manage the legal and compliance review process efficiently without it becoming a bottleneck?",
        "a": "Legal and compliance review becoming a bottleneck in a plain language documentation project is predictable and must be managed proactively. The review process itself needs to be designed as carefully as the content.\n\nEstablish scope clarity upfront:\n\nBefore any rewriting begins, I meet with the legal and compliance teams to establish clearly what they are reviewing and what authority they have to change. Legal reviews for legal accuracy — are the facts of the policy correctly stated, is the mandatory disclosure language present and verbatim. Compliance reviews for regulatory alignment — does this document meet IRDAI requirements for product disclosures. Neither team reviews for style, tone, or plain language quality — those are content decisions within the documentation team's authority.\n\nThis scope clarity prevents the most common bottleneck: legal and compliance making editorial changes that are not within their review scope, which then require back-and-forth with the content team.\n\nBatch review by document type:\n\nInstead of sending individual documents for review one at a time, I batch documents by type. All policy summaries in one batch, all claims guides in another. This allows legal to develop familiarity with the content type and apply consistent standards across the batch, which is faster than reviewing each document in isolation.\n\nCreate a pre-approved language library:\n\nFor the mandatory disclosure language and the frequently recurring legal concepts, I work with the legal team upfront to create a library of pre-approved plain language versions. \"The sum insured is the maximum amount we will pay for all claims in a policy year\" — pre-approved. This language can then be used in all documents without requiring legal review of that specific sentence each time.\n\nDefine turnaround time SLAs:\n\nWithout agreed turnaround times, legal review expands to fill whatever time is available. I establish agreed SLAs at the start of the project: standard documents within five business days, urgent documents within two.",
