        "q": "You are a content strategist at a telecom company. You have been asked to create a global content strategy for a company operating in India, Southeast Asia, and the Middle East. What are the key considerations?",
        "a": "A global content strategy for markets as diverse as India, Southeast Asia, and the Middle East requires thinking about content at multiple levels: what is universal across all markets, what needs regional adaptation, and what must be market-specific.\n\nLanguage strategy:\n\nThe three regions together encompass dozens of languages. The language strategy must define which languages each market's content is produced in, what the translation and localization workflow is, and how content currency is maintained across languages when the source content changes. In India alone, there may be a case for content in Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, and regional languages depending on the customer base. Southeast Asia adds Bahasa Indonesia, Thai, Vietnamese, and others. The Middle East adds Arabic with right-to-left text direction requirements.\n\nCultural adaptation beyond translation:\n\nTranslation converts language; localization adapts meaning. An example in telecom: a plan marketed on its sports streaming content may need different sports highlighted in each region — cricket in India, football in Southeast Asia, and regional sports in the Middle East. Financial services examples, family structures used in marketing, and cultural references that make content feel local must all be adapted.\n\nRegulatory variation:\n\nEach market has different requirements for product disclosures, consumer communications, and data handling. The content strategy must include a regulatory compliance layer that ensures market-specific documentation meets local requirements without the central team needing to be expert in every jurisdiction's regulations.\n\nContent production model:\n\nFor a global content strategy, I recommend a hub and spoke model: a central hub produces core content in a global format designed for easy localization — neutral examples, short sentences, no idioms — and regional spoke teams adapt that content for their markets. This is more efficient than each region creating content independently and more consistent than full centralization with no regional input.",
