        "q": "You are a content strategist at a fintech company. What is a content model and how do you build one for a help center?",
        "a": "A content model is a structured definition of the types of content that exist in a system, what attributes each content type has, and how content types relate to each other. It is the information architecture blueprint that makes a help center manageable at scale.\n\nWithout a content model, a help center grows organically and inconsistently. Articles have different structures, different metadata, different levels of detail, and different voice depending on who wrote them. A content model provides the structure that makes a large help center coherent and maintainable.\n\nBuilding a content model for a help center:\n\nStep 1 — Content audit and type identification:\n\nI review all existing content and identify the recurring structural patterns. Most help centers contain a small number of content types: how-to articles (procedural — step-by-step instructions), concept articles (explanatory — what something is or how something works), reference articles (lookup — tables of information, error codes, parameters), troubleshooting articles (diagnostic — if X then Y), and announcement articles (policy changes, new features).\n\nStep 2 — Attribute definition for each type:\n\nFor each content type, I define its attributes: the mandatory fields that every article of that type must have. A how-to article has: title, task goal statement, prerequisites, numbered steps, expected outcome, troubleshooting section, and related articles. A troubleshooting article has: symptom description, possible causes, step-by-step diagnosis, resolution steps, and escalation conditions.\n\nStep 3 — Taxonomy definition:\n\nHow articles are categorized determines how users find them and how the help center is organized. I define the taxonomy — the category and tag structure — as part of the content model, including rules for how articles are classified.\n\nStep 4 — Templates:\n\nI create authoring templates based on the model. Writers filling in a template produce structurally consistent content without having to remember the model requirements.",
