        "q": "You are a documentation manager at a bank. You are asked to implement a content review cycle for all published documentation. How do you design this process?",
        "a": "A content review cycle is a systematic process for ensuring that published documentation remains accurate, relevant, and current over time. In a bank, where regulatory requirements change, products update, and processes evolve, documentation that is not regularly reviewed becomes a liability rather than an asset.\n\nDesign principles:\n\nRisk-based review frequency: Not all documentation requires the same review frequency. I classify content by risk level: regulatory disclosures (reviewed immediately when regulations change, minimum quarterly review), product and pricing documentation (reviewed when products change, minimum biannual review), and stable process documentation (annual review).\n\nOwnership model: Each document has a named owner — the person responsible for initiating its review. Without ownership, documents are reviewed when someone notices they are wrong, which is too late. I assign ownership at document creation and maintain an ownership register.\n\nReview workflow:\n\nTriggered reviews: When a product, process, or regulatory change occurs, affected documents are identified and queued for immediate review. I maintain a dependency map that links documentation to the products and regulations it describes, so that changes automatically surface affected documents.\n\nScheduled reviews: All documents have a review date in the content management system. When the review date approaches, the document owner receives a notification to verify accuracy and either confirm it is current or update it.\n\nReview completion tracking: The review cycle must be trackable. I maintain a dashboard showing what percentage of documentation is current (reviewed within its required frequency), what is overdue for review, and who the outstanding review owners are.\n\nFor regulated content specifically, I maintain a record of who reviewed each document, when, and what changes were made. In a banking audit, evidence that documentation is actively maintained is as important as the documentation itself.",
