        "q": "You are a content strategist at an insurance company. How do you use customer journey mapping to improve documentation strategy?",
        "a": "Customer journey mapping is a tool that documents the complete sequence of interactions a customer has with a company, from first awareness through ongoing use. Applying it to documentation strategy reveals where customers need information and where they currently encounter friction that better documentation could reduce.\n\nHow I apply it to insurance documentation strategy:\n\nMap the existing journey:\n\nFor an insurance company, the customer journey typically has five stages: awareness and research (evaluating insurance options), purchase (selecting a plan and buying it), onboarding (understanding the policy and activating coverage), use (making claims, updating policy details, renewing), and advocacy (sharing experience or recommending).\n\nFor each stage, I map: what questions does the customer have at this point? What information do they need? What channels are they using? What are the pain points where they get stuck or frustrated?\n\nIdentify documentation gaps:\n\nThe map typically reveals that documentation is concentrated in the purchase and policy stages — product information and policy terms — and sparse in the moments when customers most need support: when making a claim, when updating their policy after a life event, or when understanding their bill.\n\nPrioritize documentation investments:\n\nWith the journey map complete, I can prioritize documentation investments based on where customers are experiencing friction. If the journey map shows high support contact volume in the claims stage because customers do not know what documents to submit, that is a higher priority for documentation investment than improving an already well-documented product comparison page.\n\nMeasure documentation effectiveness by journey stage:\n\nThe journey map also creates a framework for measuring documentation effectiveness. For each journey stage, I define what successful documentation looks like in behavioral terms — reduced support contacts, higher self-service completion rates, faster time to first claim submission — and track those metrics to assess whether documentation improvements are having the intended effect.",
